window.TEXTS=[{"id": "s1", "n": 1, "kind": "text", "lvl": "A1", "min": 1, "emoji": "1", "c": ["#2F6B3F", "#0B2A5B"], "title": {"en": "The Lost Cat"}, "audio": "audio/01.mp3", "body": ["My name is Anna. I have a small cat. Her name is Luna. She is white with black ears. Yesterday morning, Luna was not at home. I looked under the bed and behind the door. I looked in the kitchen, but she was not there. I was very sad. I asked my brother, \"Where is Luna?\" He did not know. We went outside and called her name. A little girl from the next house said, \"I can see a cat in the tree!\" We looked up. Luna was on a high branch! She was afraid. My brother climbed the tree and took her in his arms. Luna said \"meow\" and jumped into my hands. I was so happy! Now she stays in the house and sleeps on my bed every night.", "Слова из текста"]}, {"id": "s2", "n": 2, "kind": "text", "lvl": "A1", "min": 1, "emoji": "2", "c": ["#2F6B3F", "#0B2A5B"], "title": {"en": "A Surprise Birthday"}, "audio": "audio/02.mp3", "body": ["Today is Tom's birthday. He is ten years old, but he thinks everyone forgot. In the morning, his mother says only, \"Good morning.\" His father says, \"Have a nice day at school.\" Tom is sad. At school, his friends do not say \"Happy birthday\" too. After school, Tom walks home slowly. He opens the door. It is dark in the house. Suddenly, the lights come on! \"Surprise!\" shout twenty people. His family and friends are in the living room. There are balloons on the walls and a big cake on the table. The cake has ten candles. Tom is so surprised that he cannot speak. Then he laughs and blows out the candles. His grandmother gives him a present. It is a red bicycle! Tom says, \"This is the best day of my life.\"", "Слова из текста"]}, {"id": "s3", "n": 3, "kind": "text", "lvl": "A1", "min": 1, "emoji": "3", "c": ["#2F6B3F", "#0B2A5B"], "title": {"en": "My Strange Neighbor"}, "audio": "audio/03.mp3", "body": ["I live in a small apartment in a big city. My neighbor is a very strange man. His name is Mr. Brown. He is old, and he has long white hair. He always wears a purple coat, even in summer. Every day at six o'clock, he plays the piano. The music is beautiful, but it is also very loud. One evening, I knocked on his door. I wanted to ask him to be quiet. Mr. Brown opened the door and smiled. \"Come in!\" he said. His room was full of clocks. There were more than fifty! Some were big and some were tiny. All of them showed a different time. \"I love time,\" he said. He gave me a cup of tea and a cookie. We talked for two hours. Now Mr. Brown is my favorite neighbor, and I like his music.", "Слова из текста"]}, {"id": "s4", "n": 4, "kind": "text", "lvl": "A1", "min": 1, "emoji": "4", "c": ["#2F6B3F", "#0B2A5B"], "title": {"en": "The Magic Backpack"}, "audio": "audio/04.mp3", "body": ["Last week, my friend Sara found an old backpack in her grandmother's garage. It was brown, and it had a small gold key on the front. \"Let's look inside,\" she said. We opened it, but it was empty. Then something strange happened. Sara said, \"I am hungry,\" and a sandwich appeared in the bag! I laughed and said, \"I want an apple.\" A red apple appeared immediately. We were very excited. We asked for juice, a toy, and even a small umbrella. Then Sara had an idea. \"I want to go to the beach,\" she said. The backpack did not give us anything. Maybe it can give only small things. We carried it home and put it in a safe place. Now we have a secret. Every day after school, we open the backpack and wait for a new surprise.", "Слова из текста"]}, {"id": "s5", "n": 5, "kind": "text", "lvl": "A1", "min": 1, "emoji": "5", "c": ["#2F6B3F", "#0B2A5B"], "title": {"en": "A Day at the Zoo"}, "audio": "audio/05.mp3", "body": ["On Saturday, our class went to the zoo. We took a yellow bus at eight o'clock. The teacher gave each child a map and a pencil. First, we saw the lions. They were sleeping in the sun. Next, we visited the monkeys. They were very funny! One monkey took a banana from a boy's hand and ran away. Everyone laughed. Then we walked to the elephants. They are huge, and they have very long noses. A man fed them with big pieces of fruit. At noon, we had lunch on the grass. After lunch, I saw my favorite animal: the penguin. Penguins cannot fly, but they can swim very fast. I took twenty photos of them. At four o'clock, we were tired, but we were happy. It was a perfect day.", "Слова из текста"]}, {"id": "s6", "n": 6, "kind": "text", "lvl": "A1", "min": 1, "emoji": "6", "c": ["#2F6B3F", "#0B2A5B"], "title": {"en": "The Best Pizza in Town"}, "audio": "audio/06.mp3", "body": ["Marco has a small restaurant near the river. It is not famous, but the pizza there is the best in town. Every morning, Marco gets up at five o'clock. He makes the dough with flour, water, and a little salt. Then he makes the sauce with fresh tomatoes. He never uses cans. At eleven o'clock, the first customers come. They sit at wooden tables and order their food. Marco puts the pizza in a very hot oven. After five minutes, it is ready. The cheese is soft, and the smell is wonderful. Many people say, \"What is your secret?\" Marco always smiles and says, \"I put love in every pizza.\" His wife thinks the real secret is the old oven. It was his grandfather's. Whatever it is, the line outside the door grows longer every day.", "Слова из текста"]}, {"id": "s7", "n": 7, "kind": "text", "lvl": "A1", "min": 1, "emoji": "7", "c": ["#2F6B3F", "#0B2A5B"], "title": {"en": "A Snowy Morning"}, "audio": "audio/07.mp3", "body": ["Today, I woke up and the world was white. It was snowing! I ran to the window and looked outside. The street, the cars, and the trees were all under thick snow. There was no school today, so I was very happy. I put on my warm coat, my red hat, and my big boots. My little sister wanted to come too. We went into the garden and made a snowman. We gave him two black stones for eyes and a carrot for a nose. My sister put her scarf around his neck. Then our neighbors' children came, and we had a snowball fight. It was cold, but we did not feel it. After two hours, our hands were wet and frozen. We went inside, drank hot chocolate, and watched the snowman through the window. He looked happy too.", "Слова из текста"]}, {"id": "s8", "n": 8, "kind": "text", "lvl": "A1", "min": 1, "emoji": "8", "c": ["#2F6B3F", "#0B2A5B"], "title": {"en": "The Robot in My Kitchen"}, "audio": "audio/08.mp3", "body": ["My uncle is an engineer, and last month he gave me a robot. It is small and silver, and it has two big blue eyes. Its name is Beep. At first, I did not know what to do with it. Then my uncle said, \"Beep can help you in the kitchen.\" The next morning, I said, \"Beep, make me an egg.\" The robot moved to the stove and cooked a perfect egg in three minutes. I was amazed! Beep can also wash dishes, clean the table, and count the spoons. But Beep is not perfect. Yesterday, I asked for milk, and it brought me a bottle of orange juice. Another day, it cleaned my school bag and threw away my homework! My mother laughed. \"Maybe the robot is hungry,\" she said. Now Beep sits next to the fridge and waits for the next order.", "Слова из текста"]}, {"id": "s9", "n": 9, "kind": "text", "lvl": "A1", "min": 2, "emoji": "9", "c": ["#2F6B3F", "#0B2A5B"], "title": {"en": "My Grandfather's Old Watch"}, "audio": "audio/09.mp3", "body": ["My grandfather has an old gold watch. It is more than eighty years old. He wears it every day, and he never forgets to wind it. When I was a small child, I asked him, \"Why is this watch special?\" He smiled and said, \"I will tell you a story.\" Many years ago, my grandfather was a young man in a small village. One day, he saved an old traveler from a river. The man was very thankful, and he gave my grandfather his watch. \"It will bring you luck,\" the traveler said. After that day, good things began to happen. Grandfather met my grandmother, got a good job, and bought a farm. Last week, he put the watch in my hand. \"Now it is yours,\" he said. I felt proud and a little nervous. I will take care of it, and one day I will tell the story to my children.", "Слова из текста"]}, {"id": "s10", "n": 10, "kind": "text", "lvl": "A1", "min": 2, "emoji": "10", "c": ["#2F6B3F", "#0B2A5B"], "title": {"en": "The Football Match"}, "audio": "audio/10.mp3", "body": ["It is Sunday afternoon, and the big match is today. Our school team, the Red Lions, plays against the Blue Eagles. Everyone in the stadium is loud and excited. I am the goalkeeper, and my legs are shaking. The referee blows his whistle, and the game begins. In the first half, the Eagles have the ball most of the time. A tall player kicks it very hard, but I catch it with both hands. The crowd shouts my name! Just before the break, our best striker, Leo, runs fast and scores a goal. The score is one to zero. In the second half, we are tired. The Eagles score, and now it is one to one. Then, in the last minute, Leo passes the ball to Nick, and Nick scores! We win two to one. Our coach is so happy that he jumps into the air. This is the best day of my school life.", "Слова из текста", "# Уровень 2", "A2 — элементарный. Прошедшее время, короткие истории, бытовые ситуации и путешествия."]}, {"id": "s11", "n": 11, "kind": "text", "lvl": "A2", "min": 2, "emoji": "11", "c": ["#1D5C63", "#0B2A5B"], "title": {"en": "The Mystery Package"}, "audio": "audio/11.mp3", "body": ["Last Tuesday, a package arrived at our door. It was addressed to my father, but there was no name on the sender line. My father looked at it for a long time. \"I did not order anything,\" he said. He was suspicious, so he put it on the kitchen table and did not touch it. That evening, the whole family sat around the table and stared at the box. Finally, my sister suggested opening it. Inside, we found an old compass and a small piece of paper. The note said, \"Follow the direction of the needle. You will find what you lost twenty years ago.\" My father turned pale. \"I remember now,\" he whispered. \"When I was a boy, I buried a metal box near the old oak tree behind my school.\" The next weekend, we traveled to his old town, and he dug a deep hole. Someone had already taken the box, but we found a new one with a message inside: \"Thank you for waiting.\"", "Слова из текста"]}, {"id": "s12", "n": 12, "kind": "text", "lvl": "A2", "min": 2, "emoji": "12", "c": ["#1D5C63", "#0B2A5B"], "title": {"en": "A Trip to the Mountains"}, "audio": "audio/12.mp3", "body": ["My friends and I decided to spend a week in the mountains last summer. We packed our tents, some food, and warm clothes, and we took a train to a small village. The next morning, we started to hike. The path was narrow, and the air was cold and clean. After four hours, we reached a beautiful lake. The water was so clear that we could see the fish swimming at the bottom. We built a small fire and cooked our dinner. During the night, we heard a strange noise outside the tent. My friend Dan took a flashlight and carefully went out. It was only a small deer! It looked at us for a moment and then ran away. On the last day, we climbed to the top of a high peak. The view was unforgettable. We could see the whole valley, and the clouds were below our feet. I will never forget that moment.", "Слова из текста"]}, {"id": "s13", "n": 13, "kind": "text", "lvl": "A2", "min": 2, "emoji": "13", "c": ["#1D5C63", "#0B2A5B"], "title": {"en": "The Street Musician"}, "audio": "audio/13.mp3", "body": ["Every morning on my way to work, I pass a street musician near the subway station. He is an old man with a kind face, and he plays the violin. At first, I did not pay attention to him. Like everyone else, I was in a hurry. But one rainy day, I stopped to listen. The music was so sad and so beautiful that I forgot about the time. I put a few coins in his case and said, \"Thank you.\" He smiled and said, \"Music is a gift. I only share it.\" After that day, I always left home ten minutes earlier so I could enjoy his songs. Last month, I came to the station and he was not there. I felt worried. A week later, he came back. \"I was sick,\" he explained. \"But I missed my audience.\" I laughed. I never thought that I was part of someone's audience, but now I am happy to be.", "Слова из текста"]}, {"id": "s14", "n": 14, "kind": "text", "lvl": "A2", "min": 2, "emoji": "14", "c": ["#1D5C63", "#0B2A5B"], "title": {"en": "My First Day at a New School"}, "audio": "audio/14.mp3", "body": ["Yesterday was my first day at a new school, and I was very nervous. My family had moved to a different city, so I did not know anyone. In the morning, I wore my new uniform and walked to school with my mother. A teacher with a friendly smile met us at the gate. \"Welcome,\" she said. \"I'm Mrs. Clark. You'll be in my class.\" The classroom was big and bright, and there were thirty students inside. They all turned and looked at me. My face became red. \"Please introduce yourself,\" said Mrs. Clark. I took a deep breath and said my name. A boy in the back row raised his hand. \"Do you like chess?\" he asked. \"Yes, I love it!\" I answered. At lunch, he invited me to sit at his table. His name was Ben, and we talked about games for an hour. By the end of the day, I was not lonely anymore. I realized that new places can be friendly too.", "Слова из текста"]}, {"id": "s15", "n": 15, "kind": "text", "lvl": "A2", "min": 2, "emoji": "15", "c": ["#1D5C63", "#0B2A5B"], "title": {"en": "The Missing Wallet"}, "audio": "audio/15.mp3", "body": ["On Friday afternoon, Emma discovered that her wallet was gone. She searched her bag, her pockets, and even the bottom of her backpack, but it was not there. \"I had it at the café an hour ago,\" she thought. She ran back to the café and asked the waiter. \"Sorry,\" he said. \"Nobody brought a wallet here.\" Emma felt terrible. It had her bus card, her student ID, and fifty dollars. She sat on a bench and tried to stay calm. Suddenly, a young man ran toward her, breathing hard. \"Excuse me, are you Emma?\" he asked. In his hand was her wallet! \"You dropped it near the bus stop,\" he said. \"I found your name on the card and looked for you everywhere.\" Emma was so grateful that she wanted to give him some money, but he refused. \"It's nothing,\" he said with a smile. \"Someone helped me once too.\"", "Слова из текста"]}, {"id": "s16", "n": 16, "kind": "text", "lvl": "A2", "min": 2, "emoji": "16", "c": ["#1D5C63", "#0B2A5B"], "title": {"en": "A Night in the Forest"}, "audio": "audio/16.mp3", "body": ["When I was twelve, I went camping with my uncle in a big forest. We arrived late in the afternoon and quickly put up the tent. By evening, the sky was dark, and the forest was full of strange sounds. My uncle made a fire, and we roasted sausages. Then he told me scary stories about a giant bear. \"Is it true?\" I asked. He only smiled. At midnight, I woke up and heard something moving outside. My heart was beating very fast. I whispered, \"Uncle, there is an animal out there!\" He opened the tent slowly, and we both looked out. In the moonlight, we saw a large shadow walking between the trees. It stopped and looked at us with two shining eyes. Then it disappeared. In the morning, we found big footprints near the tent. My uncle said they were from a deer, but I'm not sure. I still wonder about that night.", "Слова из текста"]}, {"id": "s17", "n": 17, "kind": "text", "lvl": "A2", "min": 1, "emoji": "17", "c": ["#1D5C63", "#0B2A5B"], "title": {"en": "The Cooking Contest"}, "audio": "audio/17.mp3", "body": ["Last month, our town organized a cooking contest. My aunt Rosa, who has never won anything, decided to enter. \"I will make my grandmother's soup,\" she said. All week, she practiced in the kitchen. The house smelled of garlic, onions, and fresh herbs. On the day of the contest, thirty cooks stood behind long tables. There was a chef from a famous restaurant, and a man who made chocolate sculptures. Aunt Rosa looked at them and became nervous. \"My soup is too simple,\" she whispered. But I told her, \"Simple can be delicious.\" The judges walked from table to table and tasted every dish. When they reached Rosa, one judge closed his eyes and was silent for a long time. Then he said, \"This reminds me of my childhood.\" Aunt Rosa received the first prize: a golden spoon and a hundred dollars. She cried and hugged everybody.", "Слова из текста"]}, {"id": "s18", "n": 18, "kind": "text", "lvl": "A2", "min": 2, "emoji": "18", "c": ["#1D5C63", "#0B2A5B"], "title": {"en": "My Dream Vacation"}, "audio": "audio/18.mp3", "body": ["If I could choose any place in the world, I would go to Iceland. I have wanted to visit it since I saw a documentary about its volcanoes and glaciers. In my dream vacation, I would rent a small car and drive around the whole island. I would stay in little wooden houses near the sea. Every night, I would look at the sky and try to see the northern lights. They say the colors are green, pink, and sometimes even purple. I would also swim in a hot spring while snow falls on my head. That sounds crazy, but I think it would be wonderful. I would taste local fish and try traditional bread that people bake under the ground. Of course, the trip would be expensive, so I save a little money every month. My friends think I'm dreaming, but I believe one day I will stand on that black beach and smile.", "Слова из текста"]}, {"id": "s19", "n": 19, "kind": "text", "lvl": "A2", "min": 2, "emoji": "19", "c": ["#1D5C63", "#0B2A5B"], "title": {"en": "The Library Ghost"}, "audio": "audio/19.mp3", "body": ["Our school library is very old, and students say it has a ghost. I never believed it, until one rainy evening in November. I stayed late to finish my homework. The librarian had already gone home, and the building was quiet. Suddenly, I heard a soft noise from the back of the room. A book fell from a shelf. I looked around, but nobody was there. My hands started to shake. Then I saw a faint blue light moving between the bookcases. I was frightened, but I was also curious. I followed the light slowly. It stopped in front of a dusty book at the bottom of the shelf. When I picked it up, a small photograph fell out. It showed a smiling girl in an old-fashioned dress. On the back, someone had written, \"Anna, 1952. She loved this place.\" I put the photo back inside the book. The next day, the library smelled of flowers, and the book was gone.", "Слова из текста"]}, {"id": "s20", "n": 20, "kind": "text", "lvl": "A2", "min": 2, "emoji": "20", "c": ["#1D5C63", "#0B2A5B"], "title": {"en": "A Letter from Another Country"}, "audio": "audio/20.mp3", "body": ["Last spring, I received a letter from a girl called Mia. She lived in a small town in Canada, and her English teacher had given her my address. At first, I was surprised. Nobody writes real letters anymore! But I opened the envelope and began to read. \"Dear Yuri,\" she wrote, \"I am fourteen. I love drawing, horses, and pancakes with maple syrup. Please tell me about your country.\" I answered the same night. I described our long winters, our traditional food, and the metro in my city, which has marble columns and chandeliers. Two weeks later, her reply came with a small present: a leaf from a maple tree. We have exchanged letters for a year now. She sent me photos of her horse, and I sent her a postcard of the Red Square. Sometimes it takes a month for a letter to travel, but that makes it special. Waiting is part of the joy.", "Слова из текста", "# Уровень 3", "B1 — средний. Более длинные рассказы, эмоции, решения, работа и отношения между людьми."]}, {"id": "s21", "n": 21, "kind": "text", "lvl": "B1", "min": 2, "emoji": "21", "c": ["#143A78", "#2E6DB4"], "title": {"en": "The Tourist Who Never Left"}, "audio": "audio/21.mp3", "body": ["Many years ago, an Italian tourist named Paolo came to a small fishing town in Portugal for a three-day holiday. He expected to see the beaches, take a few photographs, and go home. Instead, he encountered something unexpected: a friendly woman who ran a tiny bakery. Every morning, he purchased a warm roll from her and chatted for a few minutes. By the end of the second day, he had postponed his flight. By the end of the week, he had cancelled it. \"I'm not ready to leave,\" he told his boss over the phone, and his boss thought he was joking. Paolo gradually learned the language, rented a room above the bakery, and began helping with the early morning work. He discovered that he adored the smell of bread and the peaceful rhythm of village life. Thirty years later, he and the baker are married. Their bakery is now the most popular place in town, and visitors often ask Paolo how long he has been on holiday. He always replies, \"Still counting.\"", "Слова из текста"]}, {"id": "s22", "n": 22, "kind": "text", "lvl": "B1", "min": 2, "emoji": "22", "c": ["#143A78", "#2E6DB4"], "title": {"en": "Escape from the Elevator"}, "audio": "audio/22.mp3", "body": ["On Monday morning, I was late for an important interview. I ran into the office building and jumped into the elevator just before the doors closed. There was only one other passenger, a tall man holding a cup of coffee. Between the fourth and fifth floors, the elevator suddenly jerked and stopped. The lights flickered. I pressed every button, but nothing happened. \"Don't panic,\" the man said calmly. \"Someone will notice soon.\" I looked at my watch. My interview started in ten minutes! After half an hour, we were still trapped, and I had accepted that I would lose the job. To pass the time, we talked. He said he was a surgeon on his way to an operation. I admitted that I was a terrible liar, which was a problem because I had exaggerated my experience in my CV. We both laughed. At last, the technician rescued us. The interviewer was understanding, and, to my surprise, the man in the elevator was the director of the company. He hired me because, as he said, \"Honest people are rare.\"", "Слова из текста"]}, {"id": "s23", "n": 23, "kind": "text", "lvl": "B1", "min": 2, "emoji": "23", "c": ["#143A78", "#2E6DB4"], "title": {"en": "The Day the Power Went Out"}, "audio": "audio/23.mp3", "body": ["It was a normal Thursday evening until, at exactly seven o'clock, the whole neighborhood went dark. At first, I thought it was only our apartment, so I went out into the corridor. My neighbors were standing in their doorways, holding phones and candles. \"It's the whole street,\" someone said. Nobody knew how long it would last. Without television or the internet, we had to invent other ways to entertain ourselves. The old man from the second floor brought a guitar and began to sing. A young couple shared the leftover pizza they were about to throw away. The children organized a shadow theater on the wall using flashlights. I realized that, in three years, I had never spoken to most of these people. We exchanged names, stories, and recipes until after midnight. When the electricity finally returned, nobody cheered. We just looked at each other, slightly disappointed. The next week, we started a monthly dinner on the staircase. It's the best result of a blackout I could imagine.", "Слова из текста"]}, {"id": "s24", "n": 24, "kind": "text", "lvl": "B1", "min": 2, "emoji": "24", "c": ["#143A78", "#2E6DB4"], "title": {"en": "A Chess Master in the Park"}, "audio": "audio/24.mp3", "body": ["Every Sunday, an old man sits at a stone table in our local park, surrounded by curious onlookers. He plays chess against anyone who is brave enough to sit opposite him. People call him \"the Professor,\" but nobody knows his real name. One afternoon, I decided to challenge him. I was fifteen and confident, because I had won every tournament at my school. The Professor smiled politely and moved a pawn. Within fifteen minutes, I had lost my queen, and my confidence was disappearing fast. \"You attack too quickly,\" he said gently. \"You don't consider what your opponent wants.\" We played five more games, and I lost them all. But each time, he explained my mistakes and showed me better moves. Gradually, I began to improve. After a year of practice, I finally managed to draw a game. The Professor looked at the board for a long moment and then offered me his hand. \"Now you're ready,\" he said. \"Next week, you can teach someone else.\"", "Слова из текста"]}, {"id": "s25", "n": 25, "kind": "text", "lvl": "B1", "min": 2, "emoji": "25", "c": ["#143A78", "#2E6DB4"], "title": {"en": "The Island Without Wi-Fi"}, "audio": "audio/25.mp3", "body": ["When my company offered me a one-month assignment on a tiny island in Greece, I was thrilled until I discovered that there was no reliable internet. \"How will I survive?\" I complained to my sister. She laughed. \"You'll manage.\" During the first week, I constantly checked my phone, although there was no signal. I felt anxious and lost. But slowly, something changed. I started to wake up at dawn and walk along the coast. I noticed things I had ignored at home: the sound of waves, the color of the sky, the taste of fresh fruit. I befriended an old fisherman, who taught me how to repair a net. In the evenings, we sat outside, and he told legends about sailors and storms. I finished my work in half the time, because nothing distracted me. When the month ended, I almost refused to leave. On the ferry, my phone began to buzz with a hundred messages. I glanced at the screen, sighed, and switched it off for one more hour.", "Слова из текста"]}, {"id": "s26", "n": 26, "kind": "text", "lvl": "B1", "min": 2, "emoji": "26", "c": ["#143A78", "#2E6DB4"], "title": {"en": "My Grandmother's Recipe Book"}, "audio": "audio/26.mp3", "body": ["When my grandmother passed away, she left me one unusual inheritance: a battered notebook full of handwritten recipes. The pages were stained with oil and sauce, and some of the ingredients were impossible to read. At first, I put it on a shelf and forgot about it. Then, on a gloomy winter day, I felt homesick for her kitchen and opened it. The first recipe was for dumplings. It said, \"Add flour until the dough feels like your earlobe.\" I laughed and cried at the same time. I attempted to follow the instructions, but my dumplings were hard as stones. I tried again. And again. Eventually, I produced a batch that tasted exactly like my childhood. I invited my relatives for dinner, and my uncle claimed that he saw tears in his eyes. Now, every Sunday, I prepare one recipe from the book. I have also started to add my own notes in the margins. Someday, my grandchildren will inherit a notebook that has two handwritings in it.", "Слова из текста"]}, {"id": "s27", "n": 27, "kind": "text", "lvl": "B1", "min": 2, "emoji": "27", "c": ["#143A78", "#2E6DB4"], "title": {"en": "The Marathon"}, "audio": "audio/27.mp3", "body": ["Three years ago, I could not run for longer than five minutes. My doctor warned me that I needed to change my lifestyle, so I reluctantly put on a pair of old sneakers and went outside. I felt ridiculous, and my lungs burned. But I kept going. A neighbor mentioned a local running club, and although I was embarrassed, I joined. The members were welcoming and never judged my slow pace. Within six months, I could run ten kilometers. Then someone suggested a marathon. I laughed, but the idea stuck in my head. I trained for a year, followed a strict diet, and gave up sweets. On the day of the race, I was terrified. At kilometer thirty, my legs felt like concrete, and I wanted to quit. Then I heard a stranger shouting my name from the crowd. It was my doctor! I smiled through the pain and crossed the finish line in five hours. It was not a fast time, but it was mine, and I earned every second of it.", "Слова из текста"]}, {"id": "s28", "n": 28, "kind": "text", "lvl": "B1", "min": 2, "emoji": "28", "c": ["#143A78", "#2E6DB4"], "title": {"en": "An Unexpected Job Offer"}, "audio": "audio/28.mp3", "body": ["I was sitting in a café, complaining to a friend about my boring job, when a stranger at the next table leaned over. \"Excuse me, I couldn't help overhearing,\" he said. \"What exactly do you do?\" I explained that I designed spreadsheets for an insurance company. He raised an eyebrow. \"And do you enjoy it?\" \"Not really,\" I admitted. He handed me a business card. It said he was the founder of a small company that built educational games for children. \"We need someone who is organized and creative,\" he said. \"Can you start on Monday?\" I thought it was a joke. Nobody recruits a stranger in a café! But my friend encouraged me. \"What do you have to lose?\" she asked. The following week, I resigned from my old job, much to my parents' horror. That was six years ago. Today, I manage a team of twelve designers, and we have released forty games. I still go to the same café and always look at the next table.", "Слова из текста"]}, {"id": "s29", "n": 29, "kind": "text", "lvl": "B1", "min": 2, "emoji": "29", "c": ["#143A78", "#2E6DB4"], "title": {"en": "The Secret Garden on the Roof"}, "audio": "audio/29.mp3", "body": ["Nobody in my building knew about the garden until I accidentally found it. One day, I climbed the narrow staircase to the roof because I wanted to escape the noise of the city. When I opened the heavy door, I could not believe my eyes. There were tomatoes, herbs, and even a small apple tree growing in wooden boxes. In the middle, an elderly woman was watering the plants. \"I wondered when someone would find me,\" she said, chuckling. Her name was Mrs. Ivanova, and she had been cultivating the garden for twenty years in secret. \"The landlord would forbid it,\" she explained, \"so I never asked.\" She offered me a ripe tomato. It tasted sweeter than anything I had bought in a shop. I promised to keep her secret, and in return, she taught me how to grow vegetables. Last summer, she handed me the key to the roof. \"I'm too old for the stairs now,\" she said. I accepted it proudly. Now the garden is my responsibility.", "Слова из текста"]}, {"id": "s30", "n": 30, "kind": "text", "lvl": "B1", "min": 2, "emoji": "30", "c": ["#143A78", "#2E6DB4"], "title": {"en": "Lost in Translation"}, "audio": "audio/30.mp3", "body": ["When I moved to Japan to teach English, I thought my biggest challenge would be the grammar. I was wrong. The real problems came from tiny misunderstandings. During my first week, I complimented my landlady's cooking by saying, \"This is interesting.\" In English, it sounds polite, but in Japanese, she understood it as an insult. She stopped speaking to me for three days. Another time, I tried to order a simple lunch and accidentally requested \"a bald monk\" instead of \"a plain noodle soup\". The waiter burst into laughter, and everybody in the restaurant stared at me. I wanted to disappear. But slowly I learned that mistakes are the best teachers. My students corrected me kindly, and I corrected them back. We made a deal: they forgave my Japanese, and I forgave their English. Two years later, I can hold a conversation without panic. I have also realized that people remember your effort more than your errors.", "Слова из текста", "# Уровень 4", "B1+/B2 — выше среднего. Сложные сюжеты, описания, причинно-следственные связи, более богатый словарь."]}, {"id": "s31", "n": 31, "kind": "text", "lvl": "B1+", "min": 2, "emoji": "31", "c": ["#7A3E12", "#0B2A5B"], "title": {"en": "The Last Lighthouse Keeper"}, "audio": "audio/31.mp3", "body": ["For forty years, Henrik maintained the lighthouse on a remote cliff off the Norwegian coast. When the government announced that the light would be automated, he did not protest. He simply climbed the two hundred steps one last time, polished the great lens, and sat down to watch the sunset. Visitors had always assumed that his job was lonely, but Henrik regarded it as a privilege. He had witnessed ferocious storms, rescued three stranded fishermen, and once spotted a pod of whales migrating south. \"A machine can turn a lamp on and off,\" he told a journalist who had come to document the occasion. \"But it will never notice when a boat is in trouble before it is in trouble.\" The journalist scribbled this down, moved by the old man's dignity. A month later, the automated system malfunctioned during a blizzard, and a cargo ship nearly struck the rocks. The authorities hastily reinstated Henrik, who accepted with a modest nod. \"I never really left,\" he said.", "Слова из текста"]}, {"id": "s32", "n": 32, "kind": "text", "lvl": "B1+", "min": 2, "emoji": "32", "c": ["#7A3E12", "#0B2A5B"], "title": {"en": "A Village Under the Lake"}, "audio": "audio/32.mp3", "body": ["During the drought of 2018, the water in the reservoir dropped to its lowest level in half a century, and something extraordinary emerged from the depths: the spire of a church. My grandmother, who had grown up in the vicinity, insisted that we visit. We parked on the muddy shore, and she gazed at the ruins in silence. \"That was our village,\" she finally said. In 1962, the authorities had flooded the valley to build a hydroelectric dam. The residents had been compelled to leave, receiving only meager compensation. She recalled the bakery, the school, and the orchard where she had kissed her first sweetheart. \"We never imagined we would see it again,\" she murmured. Walking carefully across the cracked soil, we discovered the foundations of houses, a rusty bicycle, and a fragment of a blue plate. My grandmother picked up the plate and clutched it. \"Some things survive,\" she said, \"even when everything else is submerged.\" When the rains returned, the village vanished again. But she keeps the plate on her windowsill.", "Слова из текста"]}, {"id": "s33", "n": 33, "kind": "text", "lvl": "B1+", "min": 2, "emoji": "33", "c": ["#7A3E12", "#0B2A5B"], "title": {"en": "The Translator's Dilemma"}, "audio": "audio/33.mp3", "body": ["Clara had translated diplomatic documents for fifteen years when she was asked to interpret at a sensitive negotiation. The two delegations had been quarreling for weeks over a border dispute. Halfway through the session, the leader of one side made a remark that Clara knew would provoke the other. In his language, the word he used could mean either \"compromise\" or \"surrender\". She hesitated. If she chose the harsher translation, the talks would collapse. If she softened it, she would be betraying her professional principles. In a fraction of a second, she weighed both options. Then she rendered the word as \"concession\", a term that carried both shades of meaning. The room fell silent, but nobody exploded. Later, a colleague accused her of interfering. \"I interpreted him,\" Clara replied. \"I did not alter him. Sometimes the most accurate translation is the one that preserves the ambiguity.\" The agreement was signed the following evening, and nobody ever learned how close it had come to failing.", "Слова из текста"]}, {"id": "s34", "n": 34, "kind": "text", "lvl": "B1+", "min": 2, "emoji": "34", "c": ["#7A3E12", "#0B2A5B"], "title": {"en": "The Beekeeper of the City"}, "audio": "audio/34.mp3", "body": ["Most people assume that bees need meadows and countryside, but Lena keeps hives on top of a department store in the centre of a capital city. She began six years ago, after reading that urban bees often produce more honey than rural ones. \"The variety of flowers in parks, balconies, and gardens is astonishing,\" she explains, lifting the lid of a hive with gloved hands. \"And there are no pesticides.\" Her honey, which has a distinctive flavor of lime blossom, is now sold in boutique shops. Yet the work is demanding. In summer, she must inspect the hives every week, watch for disease, and prevent the colonies from swarming. Last year, a swarm escaped and settled on a traffic light, causing a gridlock. A policeman called Lena, and she calmly collected forty thousand bees in a cardboard box while a crowd filmed her. The video attracted a million views. \"I am only a farmer,\" she shrugs, \"who happens to work on the seventh floor.\"", "Слова из текста"]}, {"id": "s35", "n": 35, "kind": "text", "lvl": "B1+", "min": 2, "emoji": "35", "c": ["#7A3E12", "#0B2A5B"], "title": {"en": "The Night Shift at the Observatory"}, "audio": "audio/35.mp3", "body": ["Astronomers imagine that their work is glamorous, but Dr. Okafor's typical night is spent in a freezing dome, adjusting a telescope and drinking lukewarm coffee. She works at a mountaintop observatory where the atmosphere is exceptionally thin and clear. One night in March, while scanning a distant galaxy, she noticed a tiny flash that did not match anything in the catalogue. She checked the equipment twice, suspecting a technical fault. But the signal repeated every seventeen minutes, as regularly as a heartbeat. She contacted colleagues on three continents, and by dawn, four other observatories had confirmed it. Her supervisor warned against jumping to conclusions. \"It is probably a pulsar,\" he said. \"We have seen similar things before.\" She nodded, but privately she felt uneasy. Pulsars don't behave like that. Over the next months, the mystery deepened. Whatever the source was, it was stubbornly refusing to conform to any theory. Dr. Okafor still goes up the mountain every night, hoping the universe will say more.", "Слова из текста"]}, {"id": "s36", "n": 36, "kind": "text", "lvl": "B1+", "min": 2, "emoji": "36", "c": ["#7A3E12", "#0B2A5B"], "title": {"en": "The Auction"}, "audio": "audio/36.mp3", "body": ["The auction house was crowded that afternoon. A rare manuscript was about to be sold, and rumors suggested that two billionaires would compete for it. Among the wealthy bidders, a modest woman in a plain gray coat sat in the back row, clutching a numbered paddle. Nobody noticed her until the bidding reached two million dollars and slowed. \"Two and a half,\" she said quietly. The room gasped. One of the billionaires glared at her and raised the offer. She countered immediately. The tension was unbearable. At last, the price reached six million, and the auctioneer hammered the gavel. \"Sold, to the lady in gray!\" Reporters swarmed around her. \"Who are you representing?\" they asked. She smiled faintly. \"The public library of my town. We have been saving for forty years.\" The crowd applauded. The billionaires left empty-handed, but one of them approached her afterward. \"That manuscript is in good hands,\" he said, and he donated a million to the library's fund.", "Слова из текста"]}, {"id": "s37", "n": 37, "kind": "text", "lvl": "B1+", "min": 2, "emoji": "37", "c": ["#7A3E12", "#0B2A5B"], "title": {"en": "The Whistleblower"}, "audio": "audio/37.mp3", "body": ["Daniel had worked in the finance department for ten years when he stumbled upon a discrepancy in the accounts. At first, he assumed it was a clerical error. But the more he investigated, the more disturbing the pattern became. Millions of dollars were being transferred to offshore accounts through fictitious suppliers. He hesitated for weeks. He had a mortgage, two children, and a comfortable salary. Speaking out would jeopardize everything. Yet each night he lay awake, tormented by guilt. Finally, he photocopied the evidence and delivered it to a journalist. The scandal erupted within days. The chairman resigned, and prosecutors launched an inquiry. Daniel was fired, ostracized by former colleagues, and branded a traitor in the press. For a year, he struggled to find work. Then the court convicted the culprits, and a newspaper called him \"the man who refused to look away.\" He did not feel like a hero. \"I just couldn't live with myself,\" he said, \"if I had said nothing.\"", "Слова из текста"]}, {"id": "s38", "n": 38, "kind": "text", "lvl": "B1+", "min": 2, "emoji": "38", "c": ["#7A3E12", "#0B2A5B"], "title": {"en": "The Deserted Station"}, "audio": "audio/38.mp3", "body": ["Halfway between two industrial towns, there is a railway station where no train has stopped since 1987. The platform is overgrown with weeds, the clock has frozen at ten past four, and the ticket window is boarded up. Yet every autumn, a man in a faded uniform appears on the platform and waits. Locals say he is a ghost, but my father, a retired conductor, knows the truth. \"His name is Viktor,\" he told me. \"He was the last stationmaster.\" When the line was closed, Viktor refused to accept it. For thirty years, he has visited on the anniversary of the final departure, carrying a lantern and a pocket watch. Last October, I decided to meet him. He was polite but reserved. \"People forget,\" he said, gazing down the empty tracks, \"that a station is not just a building. It is a promise that someone will arrive.\" I offered him a cup of tea from my flask. He accepted with a grateful nod, and we watched the sunset in silence.", "Слова из текста"]}, {"id": "s39", "n": 39, "kind": "text", "lvl": "B1+", "min": 2, "emoji": "39", "c": ["#7A3E12", "#0B2A5B"], "title": {"en": "The Experiment"}, "audio": "audio/39.mp3", "body": ["Professor Lindqvist had a reputation for unconventional teaching. On the first day of term, she handed her psychology students a sealed envelope and instructed them not to open it until the final lecture. For three months, curiosity consumed the class. Some students speculated that it contained the exam questions. Others suspected it held a prank. A few tried to steam it open, and one offered to bribe the secretary. Yet remarkably, nobody succeeded in breaking the seal. On the last day, the professor asked everyone to tear open their envelopes. Inside, each student found a single card with the same sentence: \"You resisted temptation because you valued something greater than your curiosity. Identify it.\" The room was silent. Then one student raised her hand. \"Our reputation with you,\" she said. \"And with each other.\" The professor smiled. \"That's the lesson. Integrity is rarely tested by dramatic choices. It is tested by small ones, repeated daily, when nobody is watching.\"", "Слова из текста"]}, {"id": "s40", "n": 40, "kind": "text", "lvl": "B1+", "min": 2, "emoji": "40", "c": ["#7A3E12", "#0B2A5B"], "title": {"en": "The Cartographer's Last Map"}, "audio": "audio/40.mp3", "body": ["Ivan had devoted his career to cartography, and at seventy he was preparing to retire. His final commission was a map of a mountainous region that no surveyor had properly charted. He hiked for weeks with a sturdy mule, recording altitudes and marking streams. In a remote valley, he came across a hamlet that appeared on no official document. The inhabitants, a dozen elderly shepherds, welcomed him warmly but begged him not to include their settlement on the map. \"We have survived because nobody bothers us,\" their elder explained. Ivan pondered the request for an entire night. His professional duty was to be accurate. His conscience, however, urged discretion. In the end, he drew a dense forest where the hamlet stood. When the map was published, critics praised its precision. Ivan smiled privately. \"Every map is a simplification,\" he told his apprentice. \"The skill is knowing what to leave out.\"", "Слова из текста", "# Уровень 5", "B2 — продвинутый. Абстрактные темы, рассуждения, оттенки смысла, формальная лексика."]}, {"id": "s41", "n": 41, "kind": "text", "lvl": "B2", "min": 2, "emoji": "41", "c": ["#5B2A7A", "#0B2A5B"], "title": {"en": "The Paradox of Choice"}, "audio": "audio/41.mp3", "body": ["When Marta relocated to a new city, she stumbled into an unforeseen predicament: the supermarket near her flat stocked forty-two varieties of yoghurt. In her hometown, there had been three. She stood in the aisle for twenty minutes, paralyzed by indecision, and eventually left with nothing. That evening, she stumbled upon an article about a psychologist who had demonstrated that an abundance of options can undermine satisfaction. In his famous experiment, shoppers offered six flavors of jam were far more likely to buy than those offered twenty-four. Marta recognized herself at once. Her wardrobe, her streaming subscriptions, even her dating apps reflected the same tendency to postpone commitment in case something better appeared. She decided to conduct a small personal trial. For a month, she would restrict herself to three options in every category and accept the first adequate one. Surprisingly, the constraint felt liberating. She saved hours, reduced her anxiety, and, to her astonishment, enjoyed her purchases more. \"Freedom,\" she concluded, \"is not having everything. It is knowing when to stop looking.\"", "Слова из текста"]}, {"id": "s42", "n": 42, "kind": "text", "lvl": "B2", "min": 2, "emoji": "42", "c": ["#5B2A7A", "#0B2A5B"], "title": {"en": "The Archive"}, "audio": "audio/42.mp3", "body": ["Beneath the city library lay a vast archive that almost nobody knew existed. When Tomas took the job of archivist, he inherited a labyrinth of shelves stacked with yellowing files, ledgers, and correspondence spanning two hundred years. His predecessor, a reclusive woman named Edith, had catalogued barely a tenth of it. Tomas resolved to finish the task, although it seemed futile. One dreary afternoon, he opened a brittle folder and found a bundle of love letters dated 1943. They were exchanged between a soldier and a nurse, separated by war. The final letter, marked \"undelivered\", had been intercepted by a censor and filed away. Tomas was haunted by it. Using directories, parish records, and sheer persistence, he traced the descendants of the couple. He discovered that both had survived and had married other people. He delivered the letter to the soldier's granddaughter in person. She read it silently, her eyes brimming with tears. \"He never stopped wondering,\" she whispered. \"And now I know why.\" Tomas returned to the archive, energized. There were thousands more letters waiting.", "Слова из текста"]}, {"id": "s43", "n": 43, "kind": "text", "lvl": "B2", "min": 2, "emoji": "43", "c": ["#5B2A7A", "#0B2A5B"], "title": {"en": "When a Language Dies"}, "audio": "audio/43.mp3", "body": ["Linguists estimate that a language disappears somewhere in the world roughly every two weeks. When Amara embarked on a fieldwork expedition to a remote valley, she was determined to record the last fluent speaker of a tongue spoken by only eleven people. His name was Mikael, and he was ninety-one. He greeted her warmly, offered fermented milk, and proceeded to talk for six hours without pausing. He described words for snow that no dictionary could translate: one for the sound of fresh snow under a boot, another for the blue shadow it casts at dusk. Amara realized that she was not merely documenting vocabulary. She was preserving an entire worldview. \"My grandchildren speak only the national language,\" Mikael confided. \"They say mine has no future.\" Naturally, she could not contradict him. She recorded four hundred hours of speech, compiled a grammar, and published a dictionary. When Mikael passed away, the valley was silent for a day. Yet a teenager from the village, inspired by the archive, has begun learning the language. \"A language is dead,\" Amara insists, \"only when nobody wishes to speak it.\"", "Слова из текста"]}, {"id": "s44", "n": 44, "kind": "text", "lvl": "B2", "min": 2, "emoji": "44", "c": ["#5B2A7A", "#0B2A5B"], "title": {"en": "The Forger"}, "audio": "audio/44.mp3", "body": ["Everyone in the art world revered Julian as a meticulous restorer of Renaissance paintings. Few suspected that, behind his locked studio door, he was also the most gifted forger in Europe. For twenty years, he had fabricated minor masterpieces, ageing the canvases with tea, baking the varnish, and mixing pigments according to recipes he had deciphered from crumbling manuscripts. Each forgery sold for vast sums to collectors who never doubted authenticity. Julian justified himself with a peculiar logic: his victims were wealthy and greedy, and the paintings were, technically, flawless. But guilt crept in. One evening, at an exhibition, he saw a schoolgirl gazing at one of his fakes with tears in her eyes. \"It's the most beautiful thing I have ever seen,\" she murmured. Julian realized that beauty does not depend on provenance. That night, he confessed to a detective and surrendered his notebooks. At the trial, the judge sentenced him to three years, but remarked that the court lacked the expertise to decide whether his crime was fraud or art.", "Слова из текста"]}, {"id": "s45", "n": 45, "kind": "text", "lvl": "B2", "min": 2, "emoji": "45", "c": ["#5B2A7A", "#0B2A5B"], "title": {"en": "Sleepless in the City"}, "audio": "audio/45.mp3", "body": ["For most of his adult life, Rafael had dismissed insomnia as a minor inconvenience. Then, at forty-one, he realized that he had not slept a full night in eight months. His concentration deteriorated, his temper shortened, and a colleague gently suggested that he consult a specialist. The sleep clinic monitored him for a night and diagnosed nothing organic. \"Your body is fine,\" the doctor said. \"It is your mind that refuses to switch off.\" Rafael rolled his eyes, but the doctor's prescription surprised him: no pills, only a walk. Every night at eleven, he was to wander through the deserted streets without a destination, leaving his phone at home. Reluctantly, he complied. Gradually, he began to notice things he had overlooked for years: a bakery already glowing at midnight, cats patrolling rooftops, a janitor sweeping the steps of a bank and humming an old tune. The city, he discovered, had a secret nocturnal life, gentle and unhurried. Within a month, he was sleeping again. He still walks, however, simply because he cannot imagine abandoning it.", "Слова из текста"]}, {"id": "s46", "n": 46, "kind": "text", "lvl": "B2", "min": 2, "emoji": "46", "c": ["#5B2A7A", "#0B2A5B"], "title": {"en": "The Last Typewriter Repairman"}, "audio": "audio/46.mp3", "body": ["In a cramped workshop wedged between a vape shop and a phone repair kiosk, Mr. Haddad fixes typewriters. He is seventy-six, wears thick spectacles, and claims to be the last person in the region who understands the intricate mechanics of a 1950s Remington. His customers are an unlikely mixture: novelists who crave the tactile resistance of the keys, teenagers fascinated by vintage objects, and lawyers who insist that nothing hacks a document typed on paper. Mr. Haddad disapproves of nostalgia. \"A typewriter is not romantic,\" he grumbles, adjusting a spring with a tiny screwdriver. \"It is merely honest. You cannot delete your mistakes. You must live with them.\" Yet beneath his gruffness lies a quiet pride. Last year, a famous author thanked him in the acknowledgments of a bestseller written entirely on a machine he had resurrected. The shop received four hundred orders in a month. Mr. Haddad complained bitterly, then hired an apprentice, a nineteen-year-old who had never seen a ribbon. \"Pay attention,\" he told her. \"This craft will outlive us both.\"", "Слова из текста"]}, {"id": "s47", "n": 47, "kind": "text", "lvl": "B2", "min": 2, "emoji": "47", "c": ["#5B2A7A", "#0B2A5B"], "title": {"en": "The Bridge"}, "audio": "audio/47.mp3", "body": ["For decades, a rickety suspension bridge spanned the gorge separating two villages in the Andes. Technically, it was condemned, and engineers had repeatedly recommended demolition. But the inhabitants ignored the warnings. Children crossed it to reach school, farmers carried produce to market, and a bride once walked over it in a white dress, trembling with every step. Every year, in the dry season, the community gathered to rebuild it by hand, weaving fresh ropes from grass, as their ancestors had done for centuries. When the government finally funded a concrete replacement, the villagers were ambivalent. The new bridge was safer and faster, yet the annual ritual vanished. Within a few years, the two villages, which had always celebrated together, drifted apart. Marriages declined. Festivals were forgotten. An anthropologist visiting the area observed that infrastructure can simultaneously connect and disconnect. \"The old bridge required people to cooperate,\" she wrote. \"The new one only requires them to walk.\" Last spring, the elders proposed rebuilding the old one beside the new. Surprisingly, everyone volunteered.", "Слова из текста"]}, {"id": "s48", "n": 48, "kind": "text", "lvl": "B2", "min": 2, "emoji": "48", "c": ["#5B2A7A", "#0B2A5B"], "title": {"en": "The Weight of Inheritance"}, "audio": "audio/48.mp3", "body": ["When Sofia's father suffered a stroke, she abandoned her promising career in London and returned to the family bakery in a provincial town. She resented it deeply. The ovens roared at four every morning, the margins were razor-thin, and her father, although weakened, criticized every loaf. \"Too pale,\" he would mutter. \"You rush.\" Sofia suspected that he cherished the bakery more than he had ever cherished her. One night, sorting through dusty drawers, she found a ledger spanning forty years. In the margins beside each entry, her father had scribbled tiny notes: \"Sofia's first word: 'bread'.\" \"Sofia learned to knead.\" \"Sofia left for university. Quiet today.\" She read until dawn, overcome. At six, she walked to his room and placed the ledger on his bed. He looked at it, then at her, and uttered the longest sentence she had heard him say since the stroke. \"I didn't know how to tell you,\" he said, \"so I baked.\" Sofia stayed at the bakery, but on her own terms. Her loaves are darker now, and he no longer complains.", "Слова из текста"]}, {"id": "s49", "n": 49, "kind": "text", "lvl": "B2", "min": 2, "emoji": "49", "c": ["#5B2A7A", "#0B2A5B"], "title": {"en": "The Perils of Perfection"}, "audio": "audio/49.mp3", "body": ["Ingrid was a gifted violinist who practiced six hours a day and played flawlessly. Critics admired her precision, but complained that her performances felt sterile. She dismissed this criticism until one night, before a prestigious competition, her teacher handed her a cheap instrument. \"Play this,\" he said. She was outraged. The violin squeaked, buzzed, and resisted every attempt at elegance. After twenty minutes, she lost her temper and played a furious passage with wild abandon. A string snapped. Silence followed. Then her teacher applauded. \"That,\" he said, \"was the first honest sound I have heard from you in five years.\" Ingrid understood at last. In her obsession with avoiding errors, she had eliminated everything that made music human. At the competition, she deliberately allowed one phrase to tremble. The audience held its breath. She did not win first prize; she placed third. But afterwards, an old woman approached her in the corridor, weeping. \"I have attended this competition for forty years,\" she said, \"and tonight was the first time I felt anything.\"", "Слова из текста"]}, {"id": "s50", "n": 50, "kind": "text", "lvl": "B2", "min": 2, "emoji": "50", "c": ["#5B2A7A", "#0B2A5B"], "title": {"en": "The Mapmaker of Lost Places"}, "audio": "audio/50.mp3", "body": ["Most cartographers chart what exists. Hana charts what has vanished. Her unusual hobby began when she discovered that her childhood neighborhood had been demolished to make way for a motorway. Determined to reconstruct it, she interviewed former neighbors, studied aerial photographs, and pored over municipal records. Her first map depicted every building, tree, and alley, annotated with anecdotes: \"Here Mr. Kovac repaired bicycles and never charged children.\" \"Here the pear tree dropped its fruit on the pavement each September.\" She posted the map online, and within a week, strangers were contributing memories. Soon, others requested maps of their own lost places: a drowned valley, a demolished cinema, a village erased by border changes. Hana now runs a volunteer network of historians, surveyors, and grandmothers. Skeptics argue that nostalgia distorts accuracy. Hana concedes the point, gracefully. \"A conventional map tells you where things are,\" she says. \"Mine tell you where people loved. Both are forms of truth.\"", "Слова из текста", "# Уровень 6", "C1 — свободное владение. Эссеистический стиль, редкая лексика, сложный синтаксис и идиоматичность."]}, {"id": "s51", "n": 51, "kind": "text", "lvl": "C1", "min": 2, "emoji": "51", "c": ["#8A5A12", "#0B2A5B"], "title": {"en": "The Ethics of a Lie"}, "audio": "audio/51.mp3", "body": ["Philosophers have long debated whether a lie can ever be justified. Immanuel Kant notoriously maintained that it cannot, even to a murderer inquiring about the whereabouts of his intended victim. Most of us, however, inhabit a morally murkier world. Consider the white lie: the polite assurance that the dreadful casserole was delicious, or that the haircut is flattering. Such falsehoods lubricate social life, and sociologists argue that a society devoid of them would be unbearably abrasive. Yet there is a corrosive side to habitual deception. Each minor untruth erodes the foundation of trust, and the liar gradually loses the ability to distinguish sincerity from performance. A recent study tracked two hundred volunteers for ten days and found that those who told fewer lies reported healthier relationships and fewer headaches. The authors cautiously refrained from drawing sweeping conclusions, noting that causality is notoriously elusive. Perhaps the wisest position is a pragmatic one: tact and dishonesty are not synonyms, and the skill lies in learning to tell the difference.", "Слова из текста"]}, {"id": "s52", "n": 52, "kind": "text", "lvl": "C1", "min": 2, "emoji": "52", "c": ["#8A5A12", "#0B2A5B"], "title": {"en": "The Glacier's Last Summer"}, "audio": "audio/52.mp3", "body": ["When the Swiss authorities commissioned a survey of the Rhône Glacier, the findings were sobering. The ice had receded by more than a kilometer in a generation, exposing barren rock that had not seen daylight for ten thousand years. Local hoteliers, anxious to preserve their livelihood, had resorted to an extraordinary measure: every spring, they swathed the glacier's surface in enormous white blankets to slow the melting. Visitors gawked at the surreal spectacle of a mountain wrapped like a patient. Scientists were ambivalent. The blankets demonstrably reduced ablation by up to seventy percent in the covered areas, yet they merely delayed the inevitable and cost a fortune. \"It is palliative care,\" one glaciologist conceded, \"not a cure.\" Nonetheless, she could not bring herself to condemn the effort. \"There is something profoundly human,\" she reflected, \"in tending a dying thing.\" On the final day of the season, the villagers gathered at the terminus, not to celebrate, but simply to witness. A brass band played quietly, and the ice dripped in reply.", "Слова из текста"]}, {"id": "s53", "n": 53, "kind": "text", "lvl": "C1", "min": 2, "emoji": "53", "c": ["#8A5A12", "#0B2A5B"], "title": {"en": "The Algorithm and the Poet"}, "audio": "audio/53.mp3", "body": ["When the publishing house unveiled an algorithm capable of composing sonnets, the literary world responded with predictable indignation. Eminent critics denounced the software as a soulless parody. But Elena, a veteran poet who had languished in obscurity for decades, harbored a secret curiosity. She fed the program her entire oeuvre and asked it to produce a poem in her style. The result unnerved her. It was technically impeccable, replete with her characteristic imagery of rain and ruined orchards, yet it lacked something she could not articulate. Intrigued, she spent a month dissecting the differences. The machine excelled at pattern, but it never hesitated, stumbled, or surprised itself. \"My best lines,\" she concluded, \"came from mistakes.\" Rather than resenting the technology, she began collaborating with it, deliberately correcting its fluency with awkward detours. Their joint collection earned rapturous reviews, though purists complained that no one knew who had written what. Elena shrugs. \"Authorship,\" she says, \"was always a collaboration between the writer and everything she had read.\"", "Слова из текста"]}, {"id": "s54", "n": 54, "kind": "text", "lvl": "C1", "min": 2, "emoji": "54", "c": ["#8A5A12", "#0B2A5B"], "title": {"en": "The Quiet Revolution of a Teacup"}, "audio": "audio/54.mp3", "body": ["In the seventeenth century, a humble beverage transformed the economy of an entire continent. Tea, initially a luxury imported at exorbitant expense, gradually permeated every stratum of society. Historians credit it with unforeseen consequences. Because brewing required boiled water, populations that drank tea suffered fewer waterborne diseases, and mortality declined in a way that contemporaries failed to attribute to the beverage. Meanwhile, the insatiable demand spurred ruthless colonial expansion, fierce maritime rivalry, and, ultimately, a notorious dispute over taxation that ignited a revolution. Even the humble teacup bears the imprint of this history. Its delicate porcelain, imitated from Chinese originals, prompted European craftsmen to crack the secret of ceramics, launching an industry. It is tempting to romanticize the ritual of afternoon tea as a serene tradition. In reality, every sip carries the residue of empire, commerce, and conflict. A cup of tea, properly understood, is a lesson in globalization steeped in centuries of ambition.", "Слова из текста"]}, {"id": "s55", "n": 55, "kind": "text", "lvl": "C1", "min": 2, "emoji": "55", "c": ["#8A5A12", "#0B2A5B"], "title": {"en": "A Cure Without Credit"}, "audio": "audio/55.mp3", "body": ["In 1974, a reclusive biochemist named Dr. Anselm stumbled upon a compound that inhibited the growth of a notoriously stubborn bacterium. He meticulously documented his findings, submitted them to a prestigious journal, and waited. The manuscript was rejected by a referee who deemed the results implausible. Anselm, deeply discouraged, shelved the research and took a post at a provincial college, where he taught introductory chemistry until his retirement. Two decades later, a team at a renowned institute independently rediscovered the compound and patented it. The drug has since saved millions of lives. When a journalist unearthed Anselm's forgotten paper, the institute offered a grudging acknowledgment but declined to share the royalties. Anselm, by then frail, declined to sue. \"Science,\" he said, serenely, \"is a relay race. I was merely a runner who dropped the baton.\" Colleagues found this modesty infuriating. They launched a campaign, and in 2011, a small plaque was unveiled in the foyer of his old college. It reads, simply, \"He saw it first.\"", "Слова из текста"]}, {"id": "s56", "n": 56, "kind": "text", "lvl": "C1", "min": 2, "emoji": "56", "c": ["#8A5A12", "#0B2A5B"], "title": {"en": "The Anatomy of a Rumor"}, "audio": "audio/56.mp3", "body": ["Rumors are the oldest form of media, and, as anyone who has endured one can attest, among the most potent. Social psychologists have identified three conditions under which a rumor flourishes: ambiguity, anxiety, and importance. When all three converge, as they do during epidemics, elections, or corporate mergers, speculation spreads faster than any correction could hope to travel. In the village of Orlov, a rumor that the pharmacist was poisoning her customers originated from a single overheard remark about expired ointment. Within a week, business collapsed. The pharmacist, Dana, resisted the temptation to issue a public denial, knowing that protest often lends a rumor credibility. Instead, she began offering free blood-pressure checks at the market, patiently demonstrating her competence. It took eight months for trust to return. Looking back, she marvels at how flimsy the accusation had been, and how fortified by repetition. \"A rumor is never defeated by argument,\" she reflects. \"It is outlasted.\"", "Слова из текста"]}, {"id": "s57", "n": 57, "kind": "text", "lvl": "C1", "min": 2, "emoji": "57", "c": ["#8A5A12", "#0B2A5B"], "title": {"en": "The Memory Palace"}, "audio": "audio/57.mp3", "body": ["In antiquity, orators memorized interminable speeches by imagining a familiar building and depositing each fragment in a distinct room. This technique, the method of loci, has resurfaced in the competitive world of memory championships. Tobias, a former sceptic, discovered it at thirty-four, when a bout of forgetfulness alarmed him. He constructed a palace from his grandmother's farmhouse, assigning absurd, vivid images to each doorway: a flamingo brandishing a trumpet, a sinking piano. Within a month, he could recite the sequence of a shuffled deck in under two minutes. He marvels, however, at an unanticipated side effect. The discipline of visualizing rooms forced him to revisit the farmhouse repeatedly, and long-buried details resurfaced: the smell of woodsmoke, the creak of the third stair, his grandmother's humming. \"I set out to improve my memory,\" he reflects, \"and ended up recovering a childhood.\" He now teaches workshops, cautioning students that techniques cannot substitute for attention. \"You remember what you notice,\" he insists. \"The palace is only a shelf. You still have to notice what you place on it.\"", "Слова из текста"]}, {"id": "s58", "n": 58, "kind": "text", "lvl": "C1", "min": 2, "emoji": "58", "c": ["#8A5A12", "#0B2A5B"], "title": {"en": "The Economics of Kindness"}, "audio": "audio/58.mp3", "body": ["Classical economists assumed that human beings are rational maximizers of self-interest. The notion was elegant, but behavioral research has steadily eroded it. In one celebrated experiment, participants were given a sum of money and invited to share any portion with an anonymous stranger. Theory predicted stinginess. Instead, the average donor parted with nearly a third. Even more striking, recipients frequently rejected derisory offers, sacrificing personal gain to punish unfairness. Such findings suggest that fairness is not a luxury tacked onto rational calculation but a constituent of it. Evolutionary biologists offer a plausible explanation: groups whose members cooperated outcompeted those riven by selfishness. Generosity, then, may be less a moral triumph than a survival strategy. This hardly diminishes its value. A kindness performed from evolved instinct is no less real to its beneficiary. Perhaps the truest insight is that self-interest and altruism are not opposites but accomplices, conspiring quietly to sustain the fragile machinery of civilization.", "Слова из текста"]}, {"id": "s59", "n": 59, "kind": "text", "lvl": "C1", "min": 2, "emoji": "59", "c": ["#8A5A12", "#0B2A5B"], "title": {"en": "The Unfinished Cathedral"}, "audio": "audio/59.mp3", "body": ["Construction of the cathedral began in 1248, and the architects knew perfectly well that none of them would live to see it completed. This awareness shaped their peculiar philosophy. Masons carved gargoyles on spires that only pigeons would ever inspect, and glaziers labored over windows whose brilliance would dazzle worshippers a century after their deaths. Modern project managers, obsessed with deadlines, find this bewildering. Yet historians contend that the cathedral's magnificence derives precisely from its protracted gestation. Each generation amended the plans, absorbing new styles and correcting predecessors' errors, until the edifice embodied a conversation spanning six hundred years. The work is still incomplete. One tower remains truncated, and the city has repeatedly debated whether to finish it. The current chief mason, a soft-spoken woman named Petra, opposes completion. \"An unfinished building remains an invitation,\" she argues. \"A finished one is a verdict.\" Whenever tourists ask when it will be done, she smiles and points upward. \"Ask my great-grandchildren. They will know better, or perhaps they will have learned to stop asking.\"", "Слова из текста"]}, {"id": "s60", "n": 60, "kind": "text", "lvl": "C1", "min": 2, "emoji": "60", "c": ["#8A5A12", "#0B2A5B"], "title": {"en": "The Case for Boredom"}, "audio": "audio/60.mp3", "body": ["Modern life has all but eradicated boredom, and neuroscientists are beginning to suspect that we may regret its demise. Every idle moment is now instantly colonized by a screen, pre-empting the restless wandering of the mind that psychologists call \"default mode\" activity. In this state, disparate memories drift together, unresolved problems quietly simmer, and unexpected connections crystallize. A frequently cited study assigned participants a tedious task, copying numbers from a phone book, before asking them to devise uses for a plastic cup. The bored group generated markedly more inventive ideas than a control group. The implication is unsettling: creativity may depend less on stimulation than on its absence. This is not an argument for deliberate tedium. Chronic boredom is linked to genuine distress, and no one should idealize monotony. Yet the occasional empty afternoon, unfilled and unmonitored, may be a precondition for originality. Children appear to grasp this instinctively. Left alone on a rainy day, they construct elaborate fortresses from cushions and cardboard, oblivious to the adults who fret that they are wasting time.", "Слова из текста"]}];